      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${y}^{2}+20y+87$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+87\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${10}^{2},$ writing the constant term $87$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2}}_{\u23df}-\\,{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+10{)}^{2}-{10}^{2}+87\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+10{)}^{2}-13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=10$ and $q=-13.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
