      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "If the expression ${z}^{2}+24z+138$ is written as $(z+p)^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q$?\n\n$p =$\n{{field-1}},\n$q =$\n{{field-2}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+138\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2},$ writing the constant term $138$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=12$ and $q=-6.$",
